+# Trusted Base
+
+This is the narrative source of truth for zisk-fv's current trust boundary.
+The generated ledgers under [`trust/generated/`](generated/) are the
+machine-checked audit surface.
+
+## Claim
+
+The intended soundness claim is:
+
+> Assuming the Sail-to-Lean extraction and ZisK RV64IM circuit-to-Lean
+> extraction are trusted, every state transition accepted by the modeled ZisK
+> RV64IM circuits is a valid RISC-V state transition.
+
+The global Lean theorem is:
+
+```text
+ZiskFv.Compliance.zisk_riscv_compliant_program_bus
+```
+
+Its active project-axiom closure is
+[`generated/baseline-zisk-riscv-compliant.txt`](generated/baseline-zisk-riscv-compliant.txt).
+In this checkout that closure contains 122 entries, and
+[`generated/baseline-axioms.txt`](generated/baseline-axioms.txt) contains the
+same 122 source trust declarations with source-text hashes. The V2
+`check-closure-vs-baseline` gate enforces equality between those two sets.
+
+The extraction assumptions are part of the project premise but are outside the
+Lean axiom ledger:
+
+- Sail-to-Lean extraction for the official `riscv/sail-riscv` semantics.
+- ZisK circuit-to-Lean extraction from the flake-pinned ZisK/PIL inputs.
+
+Completeness is not the current theorem. Completeness-only assumptions, when
+present, must be labeled as such and retired by future completeness work rather
+than used to strengthen the soundness claim.
+
+Out of scope: Zicclsm, precompiles, ECALL/EBREAK, and ZisK custom internal
+operations. The current opcode scope is RV64IM.
+
+## Glossary
+
+`promise hypothesis`: a caller-supplied theorem hypothesis that should be
+derived from the trust ledger or extracted circuit constraints.
+
+`promise discharge`: replacing a promise hypothesis with a derivation from
+existing trusted premises and proved Lean facts.
+
+`discharge bridge`: a lemma that connects extracted/provider evidence to a
+previously caller-supplied promise.
+
+`trust ledger`: the source trust declarations in
+[`generated/baseline-axioms.txt`](generated/baseline-axioms.txt), with this
+document as the narrative classification.
+
+`caller-burden ledger`: the generated lists of theorem and wrapper binders that
+callers must provide.
+
+`anti-laundering metric`: the generated hypothesis-count and caller-burden
+diffs used to reject refactors that only rename, split, or hide promises.
+
+`constructibility`: the requirement that any strengthened validator or theorem
+premise corresponds to witness data a real accepted ZisK trace can produce.
+
+## Current Classes
+
+| Class | Source declarations | In global closure | Removability |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |
+| Transpile contracts | 51 | 51 | Removable by replacing the per-opcode Rust-transpiler assumptions with a Lean transpiler/checker path. |
+| Memory state load bridge | 1 | 1 | Removable by proving the memory-row model directly from extracted memory AIR facts and Sail memory. |
+| Bus / lookup soundness | 28 | 28 | Fundamental to the current protocol model unless replaced by proved permutation/lookup soundness theorems. |
+| Range-bus / byte-range soundness | 3 | 3 | Removable by deriving range facts from the extracted range-bus constraints. |
+| Arith range / table / Euclidean pins | 35 | 35 | Removable by deriving Arith table, range, sign, mode, and remainder-bound facts from shared lookup evidence. |
+| Platform scope | 4 | 4 | Scope assumptions for the chosen RV64IM platform profile. |
+| Clean completeness placeholders | 0 | 0 | None are active in this checkout's generated ledger. Future placeholders are completeness-only and must not silently enter the soundness claim. |
+
+Total: 122 source declarations and 122 active global-closure entries.
+
+## Class Notes
+
+### Transpile Contracts
+
+The `transpile_*` declarations in `ZiskFv/Trusted/Transpiler.lean` assert that
+ZisK's Rust transpiler lowers each Sail-decoded RV64IM instruction into the
+Main-row shape consumed by the corresponding proof. They are removable: the
+planned replacement is a Lean transpiler or independently checked certificate
+that derives the same row facts.
+
+Current differential evidence is summarized by the in-tree transpiler harness:
+`ZiskFv/Transpiler/Static.lean` models the static RV64IM slice of ZisK's
+transpiler, and `tools/transpiler-diff` compares it against production
+`Riscv2ZiskContext::convert`. This is evidence, not proof; retiring
+`transpile_*` still requires a reviewed ledger update.
+
+### Memory State Load Bridge
+
+`ZiskFv.ZiskCircuit.MemModel.row_models_sail_state_load` connects a memory AIR
+row selected by the circuit model to the same bytes in Sail's state memory. It
+is a model-fidelity bridge, not a known bug. It is removable by proving the
+end-to-end connection from extracted Mem AIR constraints, memory-bus matching,
+byte assembly, and Sail's memory representation.
+
+### Bus, Lookup, Range, And Arith Soundness
+
+The operation-bus, memory-bus, MemAlign, Binary, BinaryExtension, range-bus, and
+Arith declarations are protocol-soundness assumptions for the current model.
+They assert that a consumer row has the matching provider/table/range evidence
+needed by the pure Lean proofs.
+
+The active ArithTable conclusion from the audit work is that opcode-specific
+facts should not be trusted when they can be derived from shared table
+membership plus finite projections. The current ledger still contains Arith
+range/table/Euclidean pins; retirement work should shrink this class by proving
+those facts from shared lookup evidence rather than replacing them with
+same-shaped axioms.
+
+### Platform Scope
+
+The four platform axioms in `ZiskFv/SailSpec/Auxiliaries.lean` fix the current
+target profile:
+
+- PMP checks are inert.
+- PMA checks are inert.
+- CLINT MMIO is disjoint from in-scope accesses.
+- Zicfilp landing-pad state updates are inert.
+
+These are not ZisK defects. They are scope assumptions. They can be removed by
+modeling the platform behavior or by proving ordinary preconditions that
+exclude those Sail branches.
+
+## Active Caller Burden
+
+The canonical `equiv_<OP>` theorem surfaces and the compliance wrappers are
+tracked mechanically:
+
+- [`generated/baseline-hypothesis-count.txt`](generated/baseline-hypothesis-count.txt)
+  records per-theorem `total=` and `hypothesis=` counts.
+- [`generated/baseline-caller-burden.txt`](generated/baseline-caller-burden.txt)
+  records every canonical theorem binder.
+- [`generated/baseline-wrapper-caller-burden.txt`](generated/baseline-wrapper-caller-burden.txt)
+  records every wrapper binder.
+- [`generated/baseline-equiv-axiom-deps.txt`](generated/baseline-equiv-axiom-deps.txt)
+  records the transitive axiom closure of each canonical theorem.
+
+The anti-laundering rule is simple: promise discharge must visibly reduce the
+caller-burden ledgers. Splitting, renaming, universalizing, or hiding a promise
+hypothesis is not progress unless the generated diffs show a real reduction in
+caller burden or a defect-scoped theorem weakening.
+
+## Defects
+
+Known bugs are not axioms. They belong in [`defects.md`](defects.md) and must
+appear as explicit theorem claim exclusions until fixed. A merged PR may weaken
+the claim only by adding or narrowing one of those defect-scoped exclusions.
+
+## Changing The Ledger
+
+After an intentional trust-boundary change:
+
+```bash
+lake build
+trust/scripts/regenerate.sh
+trust/scripts/check-all.sh
+trust/scripts/check-all-semantic.sh
+```
+
+Review the generated diffs directly. Any new source trust declaration must fit
+one of the classes above, cite the relevant PIL/Rust/protocol source, and land
+with this document updated in the same PR.
